WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.680
Good morning!

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.160
This is Georgie from BBC Learning English and today

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.520
we're learning some phrasal verbs
that you can use to talk

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.360
about your morning routine.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.840
Let's start from the beginning.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.600
First my alarm goes off.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.680
Then I wake up, but I decide

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.400
I have time to sleep in. After a while I wake up,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.000
I get up and then I put on my gym clothes.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.000
I work out.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.600
Then, after showering,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.000
I pick out an outfit to put on.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.840
I get dressed and then I say "Good morning world!"

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.160
OK. Now let's look at the verbs
in a bit more detail.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.000
'Go off' means
the alarm starts making a noise.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.360
The verb is mostly used with alarms,
car alarms, fire alarms and alarm

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.800
clocks, for example.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.400
'Wake up', a basic one, means
you open your eyes and stop sleeping.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.400
'Sleep in' means 'continue sleeping
after the time

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.880
you usually wake up'. Lots of people
like sleeping in at the weekends.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.960
Get up means stand up
after being in bed.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.400
Put something on means dress yourself in something.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.560
But be careful, because this phrasal verb can be separated by the object.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.640
You can say 'I put on my gym clothes'
and 'I put my gym clothes on'.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.640
'Work out' means 'train your body
through exercise'.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.160
Usually in the gym, but people also
like working out at home or in parks.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.160
'Pick something out' means 'choose something carefully'.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.920
This one can also be separated by
the object.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:07.280
You can 'pick out an outfit', or 'pick an outfit out'.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.360
'Get dressed' means 'put
on your clothes'.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.880
It's similar to 'put on',

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.800
but different because you're not
specifying what you're wearing.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.880
Remember, don't freak out! Just

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:24.800
come back to BBC Learning English
and we'll work on more phrasal verbs

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.120
and how to use them. See you next time.

